{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/global-palette-substitution.md", "content": "# Global palette substitution in noisy grids

A task may present a random-looking field with only two colors and no meaningful geometry. Test cellwise global color substitution as an early candidate: compare input color roles and exact output values across complete demo grids. Strong evidence is that every occurrence of a given input color maps to one output color, and the alternate input color maps to another, with no coordinate dependence. Demos can use distinct palettes, dimensions, and marker densities; compare the mappings rather than assuming literal colors transfer between examples.

Once validated, apply the mapping to every test cell without changing dimensions. Check all rows and columns mechanically. Avoid inferring a mapping from just one demo if the two colors' roles or mappings vary by example.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/global-palette-substitution.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md", "content": "# Protocol discipline and avoiding speculative fill rules

Continual ARC tasks require one valid action on every active turn, even after an interruption. A prose status report is not a valid substitute; when the instance remains active, choose among submit, request_demos, or finish according to the live counts and end-state rules. Do not make unrelated tool calls in the evaluator loop: they consume interaction opportunities without providing puzzle evidence.

After each demo, compare all changed and preserved cells before taking another costly action. In fill/color tasks, do not assume the output is a filled bounding rectangle or the complement of a flood-filled exterior. Those are competing hypotheses that need to explain every demo, including holes, boundaries, marker cells, and untouched regions. Consider directional propagation or selectively filled regions only when supported by cellwise evidence. An incorrect submission should prompt a meaningful rule revision, not a cosmetic redraw.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md", "content": "# Revising hypotheses after incorrect submissions

A wrong grid rejects the entire submitted hypothesis, but binary feedback does not locate the error. Do not respond by shifting the same speculative recoloring boundary or trying arbitrary minor variants. First compare every demo input/output cellwise and list the precise changed coordinates, including isolated endpoint changes and preserved lines. Distinguish object-internal changes from continuation/tail changes, and test predictions against all examples.

Request another demo only when you can state which live candidate rules it could separate. Otherwise derive a materially different rule from the examples and apply it once, preserving all cells not selected by that rule. This discipline prevents a sequence of unsupported guesses from spending the attempt budget.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md"}
